#!/bin/sh
# attend — which Claude Code session needs you next, shown in the tab bar.
#
# With a dozen `claude` sessions open, every iTerm2 tab title starts with the
# same glyph and the tab bar says "twelve Claudes" and nothing about which one
# is blocked on a permission prompt, which finished a turn twenty minutes ago
# and is sitting on a result, and which is still working. The need is triage —
# "what do I do next" — not an inventory of what exists.
#
# So the tab bar becomes the queue. A hook fires at each transition that
# matters and paints that session's own tab:
#
#     Notification      -> waiting  (red)     blocked on you
#     Stop              -> idle     (amber)   finished, sitting on a result
#     UserPromptSubmit  -> busy     (clear)   you are back at work here
#     PostToolUse       -> busy     (clear)   it is working again
#     SessionEnd        -> clear
#
# and a colour dims once you have actually looked at the tab. That last part
# is the whole reason this is more than an escape sequence: a tab you glanced
# at and walked away from is not new any more, but it is not dealt with
# either, so it keeps its hue and loses its shout. Red means "since you last
# looked"; dim red means "you know about this one".
#
# Nothing here names a profile, an account or a project. The profile
# directories come from the `claude.profile.*` mapping the overlays already
# supply for the claude() wrapper, plus ~/.claude, so a public clone reads
# whatever that machine happens to have.
#
# Two measurements shape the implementation and are expensive to relearn:
#
#   - A hook does NOT inherit the tab's controlling terminal. Claude Code
#     starts hook commands detached (`ps -o tty= -p $$` answers `??`), so
#     writing to /dev/tty from a hook reaches nothing. The claude process
#     itself does have the tty, and it is the hook's parent, so the tty is
#     found by walking up the parent chain — 2ms with ps, where the obvious
#     `lsof -a -p <pid> -d 0` costs ~50ms per session.
#   - Reading which tab has focus needs no Automation grant, *provided you
#     only ever talk to iTerm2*. iTerm2 automating itself is implicitly
#     allowed and a shell inside iTerm2 is iTerm2's responsibility, so asking
#     iTerm2 to name its current session neither prompts nor records a TCC
#     row. Asking System Events which app is frontmost — the obvious way to
#     write the same check — is a different automation target with its own
#     grant, and would prompt or fail on a machine that had never granted it.
#     See `focused_tty`. This is what makes a plain polling watcher acceptable
#     here where a launchd agent doing the same thing would not be.
#
# Painting is event-driven, but "you looked at it" is not an event any hook
# can see, so `watch` polls for it. It is bounded rather than resident: it is
# started by the hook that paints the first unseen tab and exits as soon as
# every alerting tab has been seen, so it runs exactly while something is
# asking for your attention and never at login.
#
# Usage:
#   attend [list]             one line per live session, most neglected first
#   attend hook <event>       paint this session's tab; called from the hooks
#                             wired in each profile's settings.json. <event> is
#                             waiting | idle | busy | end, or a Claude Code
#                             hook event name (Notification, Stop,
#                             UserPromptSubmit, SessionEnd), so settings.json
#                             can pass $CLAUDE_HOOK_EVENT verbatim.
#   attend watch [--once]     mark alerting tabs seen once you look at them.
#                             Started automatically by `hook`; run it in the
#                             foreground to watch it work. --once does a single
#                             pass and exits.
#   attend sweep              paint every tab from the session registry, for
#                             sessions that were already running when the
#                             hooks were wired up
#   attend clear              clear every tab colour and forget all state
#   attend status             profile dirs, wiring, watcher, painted tabs
#   attend dir                print the state directory
set -u

STATE=${ATTEND_STATE:-${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/attend}
TABS="$STATE/tabs"
LOCK="$STATE/watch.lock"

die() { printf 'attend: %s\n' "$*" >&2; exit 1; }
warn() { printf 'attend: %s\n' "$*" >&2; }
usage() { sed -n '/^# Usage:/,/^set -u/{ /^set -u/d; s/^# \{0,1\}//p; }' "$0" >&2; exit 2; }

os() { uname -s 2>/dev/null; }
need_macos() {
  [ "$(os)" = Darwin ] || die "$1 needs macOS — iTerm2 runs nowhere else (list works anywhere)"
}

# How long the watcher waits between focus checks. Two seconds is the point
# where the poll stops being noticeable but a glance still registers before
# you tab away; `git config attend.interval <seconds>` to taste.
interval() {
  i=$(git config --get attend.interval 2>/dev/null) || i=''
  case $i in ''|*[!0-9]*) i=2;; esac
  [ "$i" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null || i=2
  printf '%s\n' "$i"
}

# --- colours ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Base16 Eighties, the palette everything else on this machine uses: base08
# red for "blocked on you", base09 orange for "finished, unread". The seen
# variants are those two blended halfway into the terminal background
# (#1a1a1a) — same hue, no shout. Keeping the hue is the point: after you
# glance at a tab you still want to know whether it wants an answer or just
# has one waiting.

color_for() { # state seen -> "R G B" | "default"
  case "$1:$2" in
    waiting:0) printf '242 119 122\n' ;;   # base08 #f2777a
    waiting:1) printf '134 72 74\n'   ;;   # dimmed
    idle:0)    printf '249 145 87\n'  ;;   # base09 #f99157
    idle:1)    printf '137 85 56\n'   ;;   # dimmed
    *)         printf 'default\n'     ;;
  esac
}

# iTerm2 takes a tab colour as three OSC 6 writes, one per channel, and clears
# it with the `*;default` form. Written to the tab's tty device from any
# process that can open it — no AppleScript, so no permission grant, and it
# survives a full-screen TUI redrawing over the pane because tab colour is
# tab-bar state rather than screen content.
paint() { # tty "R G B" | tty default
  tty=$1 rgb=$2
  [ -c "$tty" ] && [ -w "$tty" ] || return 1
  if [ "$rgb" = default ]; then
    printf '\033]6;1;bg;*;default\a' >"$tty" 2>/dev/null
  else
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    set -- $rgb
    printf '\033]6;1;bg;red;brightness;%s\a\033]6;1;bg;green;brightness;%s\a\033]6;1;bg;blue;brightness;%s\a' \
      "$1" "$2" "$3" >"$tty" 2>/dev/null
  fi
}

# --- session registry -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Claude Code writes <profile>/sessions/<pid>.json for each live process:
# sessionId, cwd, display name, status (busy | idle | waiting | shell),
# statusUpdatedAt, and waitingFor when it is waiting. It is per profile dir,
# so every mapped profile has to be read — `claude agents` enumerates only
# the one it runs in, which is why it is not the answer here.
#
# This is observed behaviour, not a documented interface. Everything that
# reads it re-checks liveness with kill -0 and tolerates fields being absent.

# Every profile directory this machine maps, whether or not it has ever run a
# session. This is what `status` must report the wiring for: a profile with a
# settings.json but no sessions/ yet is exactly the one you are checking after
# adding it, and filtering it out would make a missing hook block
# indistinguishable from a correct one.
mapped_dirs() {
  { printf '%s\n' "${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}"
    printf '%s\n' "$HOME/.claude"
    git config --get-regexp '^claude\.profile\.' 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f2-
  } | while IFS= read -r d; do
        [ -n "$d" ] || continue
        case $d in "~/"*) d="$HOME/${d#\~/}" ;; esac
        [ -d "$d" ] && printf '%s\n' "$d"
      done | awk '!seen[$0]++'
}

# The subset worth reading sessions from.
profile_dirs() {
  mapped_dirs | while IFS= read -r d; do
    [ -d "$d/sessions" ] && printf '%s\n' "$d"
  done
}

# One TSV row per live session: pid, status, since(ms), name, cwd, profile.
registry() {
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || { warn 'python3 not found — cannot read the session registry'; return 1; }
  profile_dirs | while IFS= read -r d; do
    python3 - "$d" <<'PY'
import glob, json, os, sys
d = sys.argv[1]
for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(d, "sessions", "*.json"))):
    try:
        with open(f) as fh:
            r = json.load(fh)
        pid = int(r["pid"])
        os.kill(pid, 0)                      # stale files outlive their process
    except Exception:
        continue
    # No field may be empty, and none may contain a tab. The shell reads these
    # rows with `IFS=<tab> read`, and a tab is IFS *whitespace*: runs of it
    # collapse and leading/trailing ones are stripped, so a single empty field
    # shifts every later column one to the left — a record with no `cwd` would
    # put the profile name in the repo column and silently blank the profile.
    def field(v):
        s = str(v).replace("\t", " ").strip()
        return s if s else "-"
    print("\t".join(field(x) for x in (
        pid,
        r.get("status", "?"),
        r.get("statusUpdatedAt") or r.get("updatedAt") or 0,
        r.get("name", "?"),
        r.get("cwd", ""),
        os.path.basename(d),
    )))
PY
  done
}

# --- tty discovery ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Walk up the parent chain to the first process with a controlling terminal,
# and report both it and its tty. From a hook that is usually one hop (the
# claude process), but nothing promises a shell will not be spliced in
# between, and the walk costs 2ms.
#
# Reporting the *owner* rather than only the tty is what makes the tab state
# survive: the state file is keyed by pid and reaped when that pid dies, so
# keying it on the hook's own parent would key a tab on a shell that exits
# a millisecond later and take the colour with it.
owner_of() { # pid -> "<pid owning the tty> /dev/ttysNNN"
  p=${1:-0} n=0
  while [ "$p" -gt 1 ] && [ "$n" -lt 8 ]; do
    line=$(ps -o ppid=,tty= -p "$p" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    [ -n "$line" ] || return 1
    # ps pads both columns, so ${line%% *} and ${line##* } are empty strings on
    # real output; splitting on IFS is the only parse that survives it. It has
    # to be `read` rather than `set -- $line`, because an unquoted split also
    # globs: `??` — what ps prints for a process with no controlling terminal,
    # which is every hook — is a pattern matching any two-character name in the
    # cwd, so a project containing a `ui/` or a `go/` reported the tty as
    # /dev/ui and painted nothing, silently and only in some repos.
    pp='' t=''
    read -r pp t <<EOF
$line
EOF
    case ${pp:-} in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
    case $t in
      ''|'??'|'?') p=$pp; n=$((n + 1)) ;;
      *) printf '%s /dev/%s\n' "$p" "$t"; return 0 ;;
    esac
  done
  return 1
}

# The tty alone, for callers that already know the owning pid.
tty_of() { # pid -> /dev/ttysNNN
  o=$(owner_of "${1:-0}") || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "${o##* }"
}

# --- tab state --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One file per painted tab, named for the pid, holding "state seen tty since".
# The pid is the key because it is what the registry uses and what liveness is
# checked against; a tab whose process is gone is cleared and forgotten.

save() { # pid state seen tty
  mkdir -p "$TABS" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  printf '%s %s %s %s\n' "$2" "$3" "$4" "$(date +%s)" >"$TABS/$1" 2>/dev/null
}

# Deliberately distinct variable names: every caller is already inside a loop
# over the registry, which has its own state and its own timestamp in
# different units, and clobbering those was the first bug this file had.
load() { # pid -> sets t_st t_seen t_tty t_since; returns 1 if unknown
  [ -f "$TABS/$1" ] || return 1
  read -r t_st t_seen t_tty t_since <"$TABS/$1" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [ -n "${t_st:-}" ] || return 1
  return 0
}

forget() { rm -f "$TABS/$1" 2>/dev/null; }

# Drop state for processes that are gone, clearing their tab on the way out.
#
# The watcher does this too, but it only runs while something is unseen, so a
# session killed (or crashed) while its tab was already *seen* leaves its file
# behind indefinitely. That is not merely untidy: pids get reused, and a
# reused pid landing in the same state makes `sweep` treat a genuinely new
# alert as one you have already acknowledged — "you know about this" for
# something you have never seen. Every command that iterates the files calls
# this first; it costs one kill -0 each.
reap() {
  [ -d "$TABS" ] || return 0
  for f in "$TABS"/*; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    p=${f##*/}
    case $p in ''|*[!0-9]*) rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null; continue ;; esac
    kill -0 "$p" 2>/dev/null && continue
    read -r r_st r_seen r_tty r_since <"$f" 2>/dev/null
    [ -n "${r_tty:-}" ] && paint "$r_tty" default
    rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null
  done
}

unseen_count() {
  n=0
  for f in "$TABS"/*; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    read -r st seen tty since <"$f" 2>/dev/null || continue
    [ "${seen:-1}" = 0 ] && n=$((n + 1))
  done
  printf '%s\n' "$n"
}

# --- focus ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The tty of the tab you are actually looking at, or empty when iTerm2 is not
# the frontmost app — a tab in a background window is not "seen". Every branch
# answers with a line, so a failure is empty rather than an error.
#
# **Everything here talks to iTerm2 and nothing else**, and that is the whole
# reason this script needs no Automation grant. The obvious way to ask which
# app is in front is `tell application "System Events" to get name of first
# process whose frontmost is true` — and System Events is a *separate* TCC
# target with its own per-client grant, so that version would prompt on a
# machine that had never granted it (or, once denied, fail forever). It would
# fail invisibly, too: osascript's stderr is discarded here and the watcher is
# spawned with all output to /dev/null, so `focus` would just be permanently
# empty, no tab would ever dim, and `status` would report a healthy watcher
# with tabs stuck unseen and nothing pointing at the cause.
#
# iTerm2 answers `frontmost` itself (Standard Suite — it is not in the sdef,
# but it is real: cross-checked against System Events over repeated samples,
# and they agree). Asking iTerm2 both questions is also 40ms against 140ms,
# because it is one app round-trip instead of two.
focused_tty() {
  osascript 2>/dev/null <<'AS'
tell application "iTerm2"
  if not frontmost then return ""
  if (count of windows) is 0 then return ""
  try
    return tty of current session of current window
  on error
    return ""
  end try
end tell
AS
}

# --- hook -------------------------------------------------------------------

# Claude Code hook event names map onto the four states, so settings.json can
# pass either. Anything unrecognised clears rather than guessing: a wrong
# colour that never goes away is worse than no colour.
#
# `SubagentStop` is deliberately NOT mapped to idle. A subagent finishing is
# not the session finishing — the parent is still working — so it would paint
# "sitting on a result" over a session that wants nothing, with nothing to
# correct it until the next prompt. It falls through to clear like any other
# unrecognised event, which is the honest answer.
#
# `PostToolUse` is what stops red from lying. Approving a permission fires no
# event of its own: the tool simply runs. Without this the tab stays red for
# the whole tool run that follows — minutes, for a build or a test suite —
# pointing the queue at a session that is busy working. A tool having just
# run is the clearest possible evidence the session is not blocked.
state_for() {
  case $1 in
    waiting|Notification|PermissionRequest)          printf 'waiting\n' ;;
    idle|Stop)                                       printf 'idle\n' ;;
    busy|UserPromptSubmit|SessionStart|PostToolUse)  printf 'busy\n' ;;
    end|clear|SessionEnd)                            printf 'clear\n' ;;
    *)                                               printf 'clear\n' ;;
  esac
}

# A hook must never break the session it runs in, so this swallows everything
# and always exits 0. It also never blocks: the watcher is spawned detached
# with its output discarded, because a hook whose child holds the pipe open
# keeps Claude Code waiting on it.
cmd_hook() {
  [ $# -ge 1 ] || exit 0
  st=$(state_for "$1")
  o=$(owner_of "${PPID:-0}" 2>/dev/null) || o=''
  [ -n "$o" ] || exit 0
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  set -- $o
  owner=$1 tty=$2

  case $st in
    busy|clear)
      paint "$tty" default
      forget "$owner"
      ;;
    *)
      # Re-alerting a tab you had already seen makes it new again: the state
      # changed, so it has something you have not looked at.
      save "$owner" "$st" 0 "$tty"
      paint "$tty" "$(color_for "$st" 0)"
      ensure_watcher
      ;;
  esac
  exit 0
}

watcher_running() {
  [ -f "$LOCK/pid" ] || return 1
  read -r wp <"$LOCK/pid" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  case ${wp:-} in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  kill -0 "$wp" 2>/dev/null
}

ensure_watcher() {
  [ "$(os)" = Darwin ] || return 0
  watcher_running && return 0
  # setsid is not on macOS; a subshell that redirects everything and is
  # orphaned by its parent exiting is enough. Redirecting stdin from
  # /dev/null is the load-bearing part: a hook's stdin is the pipe Claude
  # Code writes the event JSON to, and a long-lived grandchild that inherits
  # it holds that pipe open for as long as it runs.
  ( "$0" watch </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 & ) &
  return 0
}

# --- watch ------------------------------------------------------------------

cmd_watch() {
  need_macos watch
  once=0
  [ "${1:-}" = --once ] && once=1

  if [ "$once" = 0 ]; then
    mkdir -p "$STATE" 2>/dev/null
    if ! mkdir "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null; then
      # A lock whose owner is gone is a crashed watcher, not a running one.
      if watcher_running; then return 0; fi
      rm -rf "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null
      mkdir "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null || return 0
    fi
    printf '%s\n' "$$" >"$LOCK/pid" 2>/dev/null
    trap 'rm -rf "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null' EXIT INT TERM
  fi

  iv=$(interval)
  while :; do
    watch_pass
    [ "$once" = 1 ] && return 0
    if [ "$(unseen_count)" = 0 ]; then
      # Drop the lock *before* the last look. Between deciding to exit and
      # actually dying, a hook in another session can paint a new unseen tab
      # and find `watcher_running` still true, skip spawning, and leave that
      # tab bright with nobody left to dim it. Releasing first inverts the
      # race: a hook arriving in that window sees no lock and starts its own
      # watcher, and if it arrives a moment earlier we catch it here and
      # carry on. Both orderings end with a watcher; this one cannot end
      # with none.
      rm -rf "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null
      trap - EXIT INT TERM
      [ "$(unseen_count)" = 0 ] && return 0
      # Something arrived. Take the lock back if it is still free, else let
      # whoever took it do the work.
      mkdir "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null || return 0
      printf '%s\n' "$$" >"$LOCK/pid" 2>/dev/null
      trap 'rm -rf "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null' EXIT INT TERM
    fi
    sleep "$iv"
  done
}

watch_pass() {
  [ -d "$TABS" ] || return 0
  focus=$(focused_tty) || focus=''
  for f in "$TABS"/*; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    pid=${f##*/}
    case $pid in ''|*[!0-9]*) rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null; continue ;; esac
    read -r st seen tty since <"$f" 2>/dev/null || continue

    # The session is gone: clear its tab in case it was reused, and forget it.
    if ! kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then
      [ -n "${tty:-}" ] && paint "$tty" default
      rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null
      continue
    fi

    [ "${seen:-1}" = 0 ] || continue
    [ -n "${focus:-}" ] && [ "$focus" = "${tty:-}" ] || continue

    # You are looking at it. Same hue, half the brightness.
    printf '%s 1 %s %s\n' "$st" "$tty" "${since:-0}" >"$f" 2>/dev/null
    paint "$tty" "$(color_for "$st" 1)"
  done
}

# --- sweep / clear ----------------------------------------------------------

# Paint from the registry rather than from an event. Needed once after wiring
# the hooks up, because every session already running at that moment will not
# fire a transition until its next one, and useful whenever the tab bar and
# reality have drifted apart.
cmd_sweep() {
  need_macos sweep
  reap
  registry | while IFS="$(printf '\t')" read -r pid rst since name cwd prof; do
    tty=$(tty_of "$pid" 2>/dev/null) || tty=''
    [ -n "$tty" ] || continue
    case $rst in
      waiting|idle) ;;
      *) paint "$tty" default; forget "$pid"; continue ;;
    esac
    # A repaint is not new information, so a tab already seen in the state it
    # is still in stays dim. A tab that has *changed* state since you looked
    # is new again, which is the same rule the hook applies.
    if load "$pid" && [ "${t_st:-}" = "$rst" ] && [ "${t_seen:-0}" = 1 ]; then
      s=1
    else
      s=0
    fi
    save "$pid" "$rst" "$s" "$tty"
    paint "$tty" "$(color_for "$rst" "$s")"
  done
  ensure_watcher
}

cmd_clear() {
  need_macos clear
  if [ -d "$TABS" ]; then
    for f in "$TABS"/*; do
      [ -f "$f" ] || continue
      read -r st seen tty since <"$f" 2>/dev/null || continue
      [ -n "${tty:-}" ] && paint "$tty" default
      rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null
    done
  fi
  # Anything the registry knows about but we never painted (another machine's
  # leftovers, a killed watcher) gets cleared too.
  registry 2>/dev/null | while IFS="$(printf '\t')" read -r pid rst since name cwd prof; do
    tty=$(tty_of "$pid" 2>/dev/null) || continue
    paint "$tty" default
  done
}

# --- list -------------------------------------------------------------------

# Most neglected first: blocked on you, then finished-and-unread, then the
# ones that are working and want nothing. Within a band, oldest first — the
# one that has been waiting longest is the one you have most forgotten.
cmd_list() {
  reap
  rows=$(registry) || return 1
  [ -n "$rows" ] || { printf 'no live Claude Code sessions\n'; return 0; }
  now=$(date +%s)
  printf '%s\n' "$rows" | while IFS="$(printf '\t')" read -r pid rst since name cwd prof; do
    case $rst in
      waiting) rank=0 ;;
      idle)    rank=1 ;;
      shell)   rank=2 ;;
      *)       rank=3 ;;
    esac
    # '*' painted and not yet looked at, '.' painted and seen, ' ' unpainted.
    mark=' '
    if load "$pid"; then
      if [ "${t_seen:-0}" = 1 ]; then mark='.'; else mark='*'; fi
    fi
    age=$(( now - (${since:-0} / 1000) ))
    [ "$age" -lt 0 ] && age=0
    if [ "$age" -ge 3600 ]; then a="$((age / 3600))h"
    elif [ "$age" -ge 60 ]; then a="$((age / 60))m"
    else a="${age}s"; fi
    printf '%s\t%s\t%s%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$rank" "$since" "$mark" "$rst" "$a" "$name" "${cwd##*/}"
  done | sort -t"$(printf '\t')" -k1,1n -k2,2n | cut -f3- |
  while IFS="$(printf '\t')" read -r stm age name repo; do
    printf '%-10s %5s  %-28s %s\n' "$stm" "$age" "$name" "$repo"
  done
}

# --- status -----------------------------------------------------------------

cmd_status() {
  reap
  printf 'script:    %s\n' "$0"
  w=$(command -v attend 2>/dev/null) || w=''
  if [ -n "$w" ] && [ "$w" != "$0" ]; then
    printf 'PATH:      %s  (NOT this script — something else owns the name)\n' "$w"
  else
    printf 'PATH:      %s\n' "${w:-<not on PATH>}"
  fi
  printf 'state:     %s\n' "$STATE"
  printf 'interval:  %ss\n' "$(interval)"

  dirs=$(mapped_dirs | tr '\n' ' ')
  printf 'profiles:  %s\n' "${dirs:-<none found>}"

  # Which profiles actually call us, read from their settings.json. Every
  # mapped profile, not just the ones with a sessions/ dir — see mapped_dirs.
  mapped_dirs | while IFS= read -r d; do
    if [ -f "$d/settings.json" ] && grep -q 'attend hook' "$d/settings.json" 2>/dev/null; then
      printf 'hooks:     %s wired\n' "${d##*/}"
    else
      printf 'hooks:     %s NOT wired — `attend install` prints what to add\n' "${d##*/}"
    fi
  done

  u=$(unseen_count)
  if watcher_running; then
    read -r wp <"$LOCK/pid" 2>/dev/null
    printf 'watcher:   running (pid %s), %s tab(s) still unseen\n' "$wp" "$u"
  elif [ "$u" -gt 0 ]; then
    # The watcher is the only thing that can dim a tab, so unseen tabs with
    # no watcher means those colours are stuck until the session's next
    # transition. Nothing restarts it on its own — say so rather than
    # reporting "not running" as if it were the resting state.
    printf 'watcher:   NOT RUNNING but %s tab(s) are unseen — they will not dim; `attend watch &` or `attend sweep`\n' "$u"
  else
    printf 'watcher:   not running — nothing is waiting to be seen\n'
  fi

  if [ -d "$TABS" ]; then
    for f in "$TABS"/*; do
      [ -f "$f" ] || continue
      read -r st seen tty since <"$f" 2>/dev/null || continue
      [ "${seen:-0}" = 1 ] && s=seen || s=unseen
      printf 'tab:       %-8s %-6s %s (pid %s)\n' "$st" "$s" "${tty#/dev/}" "${f##*/}"
    done
  fi
}

# --- install ----------------------------------------------------------------

# The wiring itself is settings.json, which lives in the private overlays (it
# is the file that names the plugin marketplace and the deny paths), so this
# prints the block rather than editing anything.
cmd_install() {
  cat <<'JSON'
Add to each profile's settings.json "hooks" (the overlays own these files):

  "Notification": [
    { "matcher": "permission_prompt|idle_prompt|elicitation_dialog",
      "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "~/bin/attend hook waiting" }] }
  ],
  "Stop": [
    { "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "~/bin/attend hook idle" }] }
  ],
  "UserPromptSubmit": [
    { "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "~/bin/attend hook busy" }] }
  ],
  "PostToolUse": [
    { "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "~/bin/attend hook busy" }] }
  ],
  "SessionEnd": [
    { "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "~/bin/attend hook end" }] }
  ]

The Notification matcher is not decoration. That event also fires for
auth_success, which is not a session asking for anything, and painting a tab
red for it would train you to ignore red. Matching in settings.json rather
than reading the payload keeps the hook a single `ps` and a `printf`.

PostToolUse is what stops red from lying. Approving a permission fires no
event of its own — the tool just runs — so without it a tab stays red for
the whole tool run that follows, which for a build or a test suite is
minutes of the queue pointing at a session that is busy working.

Then `attend sweep` once, to paint the sessions that were already running:
they will not fire a transition until their next one.
JSON
}

# --- dispatch ---------------------------------------------------------------

cmd=${1:-list}
[ $# -gt 0 ] && shift
case $cmd in
  list|ls)     cmd_list "$@" ;;
  hook)        cmd_hook "$@" ;;
  watch)       cmd_watch "$@" ;;
  sweep)       cmd_sweep "$@" ;;
  clear)       cmd_clear "$@" ;;
  status)      cmd_status "$@" ;;
  install)     cmd_install "$@" ;;
  dir)         printf '%s\n' "$STATE" ;;
  -h|--help|help) usage ;;
  *)           die "unknown command '$cmd' (try: attend --help)" ;;
esac
